course: politics
lesson: 20
title: Healthcare: who pays when you are ill
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: You break your arm at football. Someone has to pay the X-ray, the cast and the doctor's time. The big question is who, and when: you at the hospital desk, an insurance plan you pay into every month, or everyone together through taxes before you ever got hurt.
explain: In the UK, the NHS gives most care free when you use it, because taxes already paid for it. In the US, most people rely on health insurance, which pays part of the bills. Canada, Australia and Ireland each mix things in their own way.
explain: Every way has a hard side. "Free" care still costs money, so waiting lists can grow long; insurance gives choice, but bills can be very large for people without it. Free care for everyone and fast care for everyone are both hard to do at once.
explain: That is why people argue: about more money or better organisation, about public or private help, about who pays which share. Even doctors and nurses sometimes disagree with the government about pay.
explain: People talk about it in all sorts of slang: a Scottish gran calls a child a "bairn" and the "wee" cast a small one, in Wales "lush" means lovely, an Irish dad says "grand" for fine, and a young American might call adult paperwork "adulting" and feel "salty" (annoyed) about a bill.
fact: In the UK the NHS, set up in 1948, gives most healthcare free at the point of use, paid for by taxes.
fact: It was reported that NHS waiting lists grew very long, especially after the COVID pandemic, with millions waiting for treatment; A&E is the hospital department for urgent problems.
fact: Some say the NHS needs much more money; others say it needs big changes and more help from private companies, and some worry that this takes staff away.
fact: In the US most people get health insurance through work, buy it themselves, or get it from government programmes for older or poorer people; insurance pays much of the bill, and you may have to pay the rest yourself.
fact: The Affordable Care Act, often called Obamacare, became law in 2010; it made insurers cover people who were already ill, with a pre-existing condition, and helped many more people get insured.
fact: Some say Obamacare protected sick people and helped millions; others say it raised costs for some and put too many rules on businesses.
fact: Australia's public system, Medicare, is paid by taxes and covers much of the cost of seeing a doctor, and at many doctors the patient pays nothing at the visit.
fact: Canada's public system, paid by taxes and run by each province, covers doctor visits and hospital care; it was reported that waits for some specialists and surgeries can be long.
fact: In Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland prescriptions are free for everyone; in England most adults pay a set charge, but children, older people and some others get them free.
fact: Devolution means parts of the UK, like Scotland and Wales, make some of their own decisions, including about health.
fact: It was reported that in many parts of England it became very hard to find an NHS dentist taking new patients, so some people paid privately and some went without.
fact: It was reported that resident doctors in England went on strike several times over pay, saying it had fallen behind prices; the government said it could not afford what they asked, and emergency care continued.
fact: In Ireland a visit to a GP, the family doctor, is not free for everyone; it was reported that young children and some other groups can see a GP for free.
fact: People disagree about how much healthcare should be public or private, and many countries mix both.
exercise: When was the NHS set up? | 1948 | 1918 | 1998 | 2010
exercise: What does "free at the point of use" mean? | You don't pay when you get the care, because taxes already paid for it | Doctors and nurses work for no pay at all, out of kindness | Medicine costs nothing to make, so nobody needs to pay for it | Only children are treated without paying; everyone else pays
exercise: What was reported about NHS waiting lists after the COVID pandemic? | They grew very long, with millions waiting | They disappeared completely within a few weeks of the pandemic ending | Nobody had to wait at all, because hospitals were empty | They only affected people waiting for a dentist
exercise: What is A&E? | The hospital department for urgent problems | A strong medicine given for pain and fever | A degree that every doctor must study for | A type of health insurance you buy each year
exercise: How do most people in the US pay for healthcare? | Through health insurance | Through the NHS, the same as in Britain | Hospitals in the US are always free for everyone | With coins and notes handed over at the hospital door
exercise: Who pays for the NHS? | Everyone together, through taxes | Each patient, at the hospital desk | The King, from his own money | Private insurance companies
exercise: What did the Affordable Care Act make insurers do? | Cover people even if they were already ill | Charge people who are already sick double the price | Close down all the hospitals in poorer areas | Stop selling health insurance to anyone at all
exercise: What is a pre-existing condition? | An illness you already have before getting insurance | An illness you catch while you are staying in hospital | A rule that doctors must follow before an operation | A broken bone from playing football at school
exercise: What is Medicare in Australia? | A public system paid by taxes that covers much of the cost of seeing a doctor | A private club for doctors who meet to share their news | A medicine you can buy for colds and coughs | A large US insurance company that sells cover in Australia
exercise: Why can "free" healthcare still have long waits? | It still costs money, and there may not be enough staff and beds | Doctors refuse to work unless people pay | Free care is only given on Sundays | Patients must queue to pay their bills
exercise: Who runs each health system in Canada? | Each province | The King alone, from London | Private companies only, for profit | The US government in Washington
exercise: What is a common complaint about Canada's system, as reported? | Waits for some specialists and surgeries can be long | It charges every patient for every single visit | Only rich people are allowed to use it at all | It has no hospitals, so people must go to the US
exercise: Where are prescriptions free for everyone? | Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland | England only, and nowhere else in the UK | Nowhere in the UK at all | Only in London and its nearby towns
exercise: What is devolution? | When parts of the UK make some of their own decisions | When a doctor goes away on a long holiday | When the taxes that everyone pays go down | When the NHS closes and private hospitals take over
exercise: What was reported about NHS dentists in many parts of England? | Many could not take new NHS patients | Every dentist in England became completely free for everyone | Dentists were banned from treating children | Nobody needed a dentist because teeth got stronger
exercise: Why did resident doctors in England strike, as reported? | They said their pay had fallen behind prices | They wanted to see far fewer patients each day and go home early | They were told to strike by their own patients | They wanted to close every A&E department
exercise: In Ireland, who was reported to get free GP visits? | Young children and some other groups | Every person in the country, whatever their age | Nobody at all, because GPs always charge | Only tourists who are visiting for a holiday
test: Oliver in Glasgow: "broke ma arm, got a wee cast, and naebody asked for a penny!" His American cousin says "so it's totally free??" What is true? | It is paid by taxes in advance, so it is free when you use it | Doctors in Scotland work for nothing, because they love the job | The hospital simply forgot to send the bill this time | Only Scots pay double later on, when they grow up
test: Hannah's gran in Wales has waited over a year for a hip operation. "It's not lush, is it," she says (lush = lovely). What would happen if the NHS had more staff and beds? | Waiting lists could get shorter, though some say better organisation matters too | Everyone would have to start paying for every visit to a doctor | Waiting lists would get even longer, because more staff means more paperwork | Hips would heal by themselves without any operation
test: «twisted my ankle at soccer, mom's on the phone like "is this covered??" adulting is wild» (adulting = grown-up tasks). Why does his mom ask? | Insurance pays part of US bills, so she checks what it covers | Hospitals in Ohio are always free, so she is just being polite | The insurance company is the doctor who will treat him | She wants a discount on sweets from the shop
test: Kai's aunt says "Obamacare saved me"; his uncle is salty because his costs went up (salty = annoyed). What is the best way to see it? | The same law can help one person and cost another, so both stories are real | Only the aunt can be telling the truth, because laws only ever help people | Only the uncle can be telling the truth | The law has never been used by anyone
test: In the arvo (afternoon), Aroha's Aussie cousin waves a green card: "Medicare, so the doc costs less." Who pays for that? | Australian taxpayers, through the public system | The doctor, out of her own pocket | A US insurance company, through her card | Nobody, because doctors' visits cost nothing to run
test: Ethan's grandpa in Toronto paid nothing for heart surgery, but his mum waited months for a knee specialist. What does this show? | Free care for everyone and fast care for everyone are hard to do at once | Canada charges for heart surgery but not for knees | Specialists in Canada are free and you can always see them on the same day | Knees are not covered by any health system anywhere
test: Chloe in Cardiff: "We don't pay for prescriptions." Her English friend's mum did pay. Who is right? | Both: Wales made a different rule from England | Only Chloe, because prescriptions are free in England too | Only the friend, because Wales charges more than England does | Neither, because nobody pays for prescriptions anywhere
test: «rang 12 dentists, none take NHS, gonna have to go private, gutted» What was reported about this? | Many parts of England had very few NHS dentists taking new patients | All private dentists are free, so going private costs nothing | NHS dentists only treat adults and never children | Dentists in England closed for good
test: Amira's dad sees doctors holding signs saying "Pay restoration": "Even doctors strike?" What are they asking for? | Pay brought back up to what it was worth in the past | Shorter hospital corridors and smaller car parks | Free sweets and drinks for every patient on the ward | An end to A&E so that they can work fewer nights
test: Oisin in Dublin: "grand, the young one's ear infection cost nothing, but Mam paid when I went!" (grand = fine). What explains it? | Free GP visits were extended to young children | GP visits became free for every person in Ireland | Oisin's GP was a tourist, so he did not charge | Ear infections are always free to treat, wherever you live
test: A clever riddle: Country A pays all care from taxes; Country B leaves it to private insurance. Which problem is each most likely to argue about? | A about waiting lists, B about big bills for people without cover | A about big bills for people without cover, B about long waiting lists | Both of them argue only about the weather | Neither has any arguments, because both systems work perfectly
test: "Healthcare should be totally free AND super fast AND cost no taxes" says a mate. What is the flaw? | Free care is paid for by taxes, and fast care needs enough staff and money | Healthcare costs nothing to run, so there is no flaw | Taxes do not pay for hospitals; hospitals pay for themselves by selling medicine | Doctors never need paying, because they work for free
exam: Identify the year in which the National Health Service of the United Kingdom was established. | Nineteen hundred and forty-eight | Nineteen hundred and eighteen | Nineteen hundred and ninety-eight | Two thousand and ten
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the principle "free at the point of use"? | Care is provided without charge at the time of treatment, the cost having been met in advance through taxation | Practitioners provide care without receiving remuneration, the service being entirely voluntary | The production of medicine incurs no cost to any party | Care is provided without charge to visitors only, whereas residents pay in full at the time of treatment
exam: Select the response consistent with the established provisions of the Affordable Care Act of 2010 in respect of persons already suffering from an illness. | Insurers are required to provide cover notwithstanding a pre-existing condition | Insurers are permitted to refuse cover to all such persons without explanation | Such persons are obliged to pay double premiums | Such persons are excluded from all hospitals
exam: Whereas prescriptions in Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland are dispensed without charge, determine the arrangement which applies to most adults in England. | Most adults pay a fixed charge, whereas children and certain other groups are exempt | All persons in England receive prescriptions without charge, irrespective of age or circumstance | Only children in England are required to pay | Prescriptions are unavailable in England
